The globalization of integrated circuit (IC) manufacturing has lured untrusted third-party entities. Logic locking, a carefully devised, key-driven approach for protection of design intellectual property (IP), is commonly used to secure ICs. Of late, large language models (LLMs) have disrupted every possible research area, including hardware security. In GLLaMoR [8], a baseline LLM was used to identify influential gates in a circuit and lock them. Here, we highlight reliability issues when using baseline LLMs to identify influential gates. We overcome these limitations by proposing an agent-assisted LLM framework, called Hector, that enables users to lock circuits using their preferred locking scheme with simple prompts. Additionally, Hector ensures the resiliency of the locked ICs through rigorous equivalence and SAT-attack tests.